=== lesson
id: ds-linked
title: Linked lists
summary: A linked list keeps each item in its own node, anywhere in memory, and links every node to the next one. Getting to item i means following i links, but adding or removing at the front, or next to a node you're holding, only changes a link or two. You'll build and walk linked lists, compare them with arrays operation by operation, and meet the three kinds you'll study next: singly, doubly and circular.

=== learn
### Nodes and links
An array keeps its items side by side in one block. A **linked list** doesn't: each item lives in its own small object, a **node**, which can be anywhere in memory. Each node holds its item and a **link** to the next node: a reference that says where the next node is. The list only has to remember where the first node is, which is called the **head**. The last node's link is `None`: that marks the end.

| Node at address | Holds | Link to the next node |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 2040 | 17 | 1100 |
| 1100 | 42 | 3300 |
| 3300 | 8 | None |

The nodes are scattered (2040, 1100, 3300), but following the links from the head visits them in order: 17, then 42, then 8. You met the node class in the Classes lesson:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

head = Node(17, Node(42, Node(8)))
print(head.val, head.next.val, head.next.next.val)
print(head.next.next.next)
```
```output
17 42 8
None
```
Python makes `Node(8)` first (the innermost call), then `Node(42, ...)` linking to it, then `Node(17, ...)`. `head` is the first node, and every other node is reached from it.

### Walking the list
There's no formula for where item i is, because the nodes aren't evenly spaced. The only way to reach a node is to start at the head and follow the links, one at a time:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

def get(head, i):
    node = head
    for step in range(i):
        node = node.next
    return node.val

def to_list(head):
    items = []
    node = head
    while node is not None:
        items.append(node.val)
        node = node.next
    return items

head = Node(17, Node(42, Node(8)))
print(get(head, 2))
print(to_list(head))
```
```output
8
[17, 42, 8]
```
`get(head, 2)` follows 2 links. Reaching item i takes i steps: O(i), and O(n) for the last item, where an array needs one step, O(1). `to_list` visits every node, O(n), and is a handy way to print a linked list. Animation 1 walks to an item.

### Inserting at the front: O(1)
Adding an item at the front of an array shifts every item one place right: O(n). In a linked list nothing moves. Make a new node that links to the old head, and make the new node the head:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

head = Node(42, Node(8))
head = Node(17, head)
print(head.val, head.next.val, head.next.next.val)
```
```output
17 42 8
```
`Node(17, head)` makes a node that links to the old first node, 42. Then `head = ...` moves the head to the new node. That's the same two steps however long the list is: O(1). Removing the first node is O(1) too: `head = head.next` moves the head past it.

### Inserting and deleting after a node: O(1)
When you're already holding a node, you can insert a new node right after it by changing two links. Nothing else moves:

1. `new = Node(99, node.next)`: the new node links to the node that used to come after `node`.
2. `node.next = new`: `node` now links to the new node.

The order matters. If `node.next = new` ran first, the link to the rest of the list would be overwritten before the new node had copied it, and the rest of the list would be lost. To **delete** the node after `node`, link around it: `node.next = node.next.next`. Nothing links to the skipped node any more, so Python frees its memory.
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

def to_list(head):
    items = []
    node = head
    while node is not None:
        items.append(node.val)
        node = node.next
    return items

head = Node(17, Node(42, Node(8)))
node = head.next
new = Node(99, node.next)
node.next = new
print(to_list(head))
head.next = head.next.next
print(to_list(head))
```
```output
[17, 42, 99, 8]
[17, 99, 8]
```
The link changes are O(1). But to **get to** the node at index i you first walk i links, so inserting at index i costs O(i) for the walk and O(1) for the links. A linked list is fast at changing things where you already are, and slow at getting somewhere. Animation 2 shows an insert.

### Arrays and linked lists compared

| Operation | Array (Python `list`) | Linked list |
| --- | --- | --- |
| read or write item i | O(1) | O(i): walk from the head |
| insert or delete at the front | O(n): every item shifts | O(1): change the head |
| insert or delete next to a node you hold | O(n): the items after it shift | O(1): change one or two links |
| add at the end | O(1) amortized | O(n) to walk there, or O(1) if you also keep a `tail` link to the last node |
| search for a value | O(n), or O(log n) with binary search if sorted | O(n), even if sorted: there's no jumping to the middle |
| extra memory | some spare capacity | one link (or two) for every item |

Two more differences matter in practice. An array's items sit side by side, which a computer reads very fast, while a linked list's nodes are scattered, so walking a linked list is slower even though both walks are O(n). And a linked list never has to copy all its items to grow: each new node is a small separate piece of memory.

### Three kinds of linked list
The next three lessons look at each kind in depth:

| Kind | Each node has | Where it ends | Walk backwards? | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **singly linked** | `val`, `next` | the last node's `next` is None | no | stacks, hash map buckets, simple lists |
| **doubly linked** | `val`, `prev`, `next` | the first node's `prev` and the last node's `next` are None | yes, along `prev` | browser history, undo and redo, caches |
| **circular** | `val`, `next` (and `prev`, if it's doubly linked) | it doesn't: the last node links back to the first | only if doubly linked | taking turns, playlists on repeat |

A circular list has no None at the end, so a walk round it never stops by itself. To go round once, remember the node you started from and stop when you're back at it. `node is start` asks whether `node` and `start` are the very same node:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

a = Node("Ann")
b = Node("Ben")
c = Node("Cy")
a.next = b
b.next = c
c.next = a

node = a
for turn in range(5):
    print(node.val, end=" ")
    node = node.next
print()

start = b
node = start
while True:
    print(node.val, end=" ")
    node = node.next
    if node is start:
        break
print()
```
```output
Ann Ben Cy Ann Ben
Ben Cy Ann
```
The first loop shows the turns going round and round: after Cy comes Ann again, with no end to fall off. The second loop goes round exactly once: it starts at Ben and stops as soon as `node is start`, back at Ben.

### What the extra link buys
A doubly linked node knows both of its neighbours, so a doubly linked list can do two things quickly that a singly linked list can't. Both columns assume the list keeps a `head` and a `tail`:

| Operation | Singly linked | Doubly linked |
| --- | --- | --- |
| insert or delete at the front | O(1) | O(1) |
| insert at the end | O(1) | O(1) |
| delete the last node | O(n): walk to find the node before it | O(1): it's `tail.prev` |
| delete a node you hold | O(n): walk to find the node before it | O(1): its `prev` and `next` are both known |
| walk backwards | not possible | O(n), along `prev` |
| links per node | 1 | 2 |

Deleting a node means changing the link of the node **before** it. A singly linked node doesn't know which node that is, so you have to walk from the head to find it. A doubly linked node has it in `prev`. The price is a second link in every node, and twice as many links to keep right on every insert and delete. A circular list costs the same as the singly or doubly linked list it's made from; what it adds is a way back to the start.

### When a linked list beats an array
A linked list is the better choice when you:

- insert and delete at the front a lot, as in a stack or a queue,
- insert and delete at a place you're already holding, like the cursor of a text editor or the song that's playing in a playlist,
- never need item i by its index.

An array is the better choice when you:

- read items by index, or use binary search,
- mostly walk through the items in order, which is faster when they sit side by side,
- want to use as little memory as possible per item.

In Python, `list` is an array, and you'll rarely need a hand-made linked list for everyday programs. But linked nodes are the building block of the stacks, queues, hash maps, trees and graphs in the rest of this unit, and Python's `collections.deque`, coming up in the Queues lesson, is built from linked blocks.

=== keypoints
- A linked list keeps each item in its own **node**, with a link to the next node. The list keeps the **head**; the last node's `next` is None.
- Reaching item i means following i links from the head: O(i), and O(n) at worst. There's no address formula, so no binary search.
- Inserting or deleting at the front is O(1): `head = Node(x, head)` and `head = head.next`.
- Holding a node, inserting after it (`new = Node(x, node.next)`, then `node.next = new`) or deleting after it (`node.next = node.next.next`) is O(1). Getting to the node is the slow part.
- Singly linked nodes have `next`; doubly linked nodes have `prev` and `next`, so they can walk backwards and delete a node they hold in O(1); a circular list's last node links back to the first.
- Choose a linked list for lots of inserts and deletes at the front or at a place you hold. Choose an array for access by index, binary search and fast walking.

=== example: Counting the links
intro: A list built by inserting at the front, then three items fetched by walking, counting the links followed for each one.
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

def get(head, i):
    node = head
    links = 0
    for step in range(i):
        node = node.next
        links += 1
    return node.val, links

head = None
for x in [50, 40, 30, 20, 10]:
    head = Node(x, head)

for i in [0, 2, 4]:
    print(i, get(head, i))
```
1-4: The node class: a value and a link to the next node.
6-12: Walk i links from the head, counting them, and return the value with the count.
14-16: Start with an empty list (head is None) and insert at the front five times. The last value inserted, 10, ends up first: the list is 10, 20, 30, 40, 50.
18-19: Item i costs i links: O(i). An array would reach any of them in one step.

=== example: Front inserts, array against linked list
intro: Inserting n items at the front, one by one. The array counts how many items it shifts; the linked list counts how many links it changes.
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

def array_front_inserts(n):
    arr = []
    moves = 0
    for x in range(n):
        moves += len(arr)
        arr.insert(0, x)
    return moves

def linked_front_inserts(n):
    head = None
    changes = 0
    for x in range(n):
        head = Node(x, head)
        changes += 1
    return changes

for n in [10, 100, 1000]:
    print(n, array_front_inserts(n), linked_front_inserts(n))
```
6-12: Each insert at the front of an array shifts every item that's already there: `len(arr)` moves.
14-19: Each insert at the front of a linked list makes one node and moves the head: one change, whatever the length.
21-22: The array makes 0 + 1 + ... + (n − 1) = n(n − 1) / 2 moves, O(n²) in all. The linked list makes n changes, O(n).

=== animation: Walking to item 3
view: linked head ptr=node
intro: There's no formula for where item 3 is. Watch `node` start at the head and follow three links to get there.
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

head = Node(17, Node(42, Node(8, Node(23))))
node = head
for step in range(3):
    node = node.next
print(node.val)
```
1: The node class: a value and a link to the next node.
3: Store this node's value, {val}.
4: Store the link: {'None, so this is the last node' if next is None else 'the node holding ' + str(next.val)}.
6: Build four nodes, from the inside out: 23, then 8, then 42, then 17. head is the first one.
7: Start at the head.
8: Take the next step: 3 steps in all.
9: Follow the link from {node.val} to {node.next.val}.
10: Three links later, node holds item 3: {node.val}. Item i takes i steps: O(i).

=== animation: Inserting after a node
view: linked head ptr=node,new
linked new
intro: We hold the node with 42 and insert 99 right after it. Watch the two links change, in the right order, while every node stays where it is.
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

head = Node(17, Node(42, Node(8)))
node = head.next
new = Node(99)
new.next = node.next
node.next = new
print(head.val, head.next.val, head.next.next.val, head.next.next.next.val)
```
1: The node class: a value and a link to the next node.
3: Store this node's value, {val}.
4: Store the link to the next node.
6: A list of three nodes: 17, 42, 8.
7: We're holding the node with 42.
8: Make the new node. Nothing links to it yet, and it links to nothing.
9: Step 1: the new node links to the node after 42, which holds {node.next.val}.
10: Step 2: 42 links to the new node. Now 99 is in the list.
11: Two links changed and nothing moved: O(1).

=== task: predict easy
title: Following the links
code:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

head = Node(4, Node(9, Node(2)))
print(head.val, head.next.val)
print(head.next.next.next)
```
wrong[4 9\n2]: `head.next.next` is the node with 2. One more `.next` reads that node's link, which is None: the end of the list.
wrong[9 2\nNone]: `head` is the first node, which holds 4. `head.next` is the second node, 9.
hint: `head` holds 4, and each `.next` moves one node along.
hint: The last node, 2, links to None.
right: `head` is 4 and `head.next` is 9. Three `.next`s go past the last node (2) to its link: None.
wrong: `head` → 4, `head.next` → 9, `head.next.next` → 2, and `head.next.next.next` is the last node's link: None.

=== task: mcq easy
title: Why O(i)?
prompt: Why does reading item i of a linked list take O(i) steps, when an array takes O(1)?
* The nodes can be anywhere in memory, so the only way to reach node i is to follow i links from the head :: Right. There's no address formula, because the nodes aren't evenly spaced.
- Each node holds a big object, which is slow to read :: The size of the item doesn't matter: reading one node is O(1). Getting to it is the slow part.
- The list has to be sorted first :: Nothing gets sorted. The walk is needed in a sorted list too.
- Python checks every node to make sure the list isn't broken :: Python doesn't check anything. Following the links is the only way to find node i.
hint: How does an array find item i?
hint: Is there a formula for where node i is?
right: An array works out where item i is with one multiplication. A linked list's nodes are scattered, so it has to follow the links one by one.
wrong: The nodes aren't side by side, so no formula gives node i's address. Starting at the head, it takes i links to get there.

=== task: fill easy
title: A new head
prompt: Fill in the blank so that 17 is added at the front of the list, and the program prints:

```output
17 42 8
```
code:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

head = Node(42, Node(8))
head = Node(17, ??)
print(head.val, head.next.val, head.next.next.val)
```
answer: head
wrong[head.next]: `head.next` is the node with 8, so 17 would skip the 42: the list would be 17, 8.
wrong[None]: That makes 17 a list on its own: its link is None, and the old nodes are lost.
hint: The new node should link to the node that's first right now.
hint: Which name holds the first node?
right: `Node(17, head)` links the new node to the old first node, then `head = ...` makes it the first node: O(1), and nothing moves.
wrong: The new node must link to the old head, so the blank is `head`. The list becomes 17, 42, 8.

=== task: code easy
title: Count the nodes
prompt: Write `length(head)` that returns how many nodes a linked list has. An empty list has head None and length 0. The starter includes the `Node` class.

For example, `length(Node(1, Node(2, Node(3))))` returns 3.
starter:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

def length(head):
    count = 0
    # your code here
    return count
```
solution:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

def length(head):
    count = 0
    node = head
    while node is not None:
        count += 1
        node = node.next
    return count
```
tests:
- length(Node(1, Node(2, Node(3))))
- length(None)
- length(Node("a"))
- length(Node(5, Node(5, Node(5, Node(5)))))
fail[1]: Three nodes: 1, 2 and 3. Walk from the head and add 1 for every node until you reach None.
fail[3]: One node, whose link is None: length 1.
error[AttributeError]: You went past the end of the list. Stop when `node` is None: `while node is not None:`.
hint: Start with `node = head`.
hint: `while node is not None:`, add 1, then move on with `node = node.next`.
right: Every node is visited once: O(n). A Python list stores its length, so `len` is O(1), but a plain linked list has to count.
wrong: Walk from the head: while `node` is not None, add 1 and move to `node.next`. Return the count.

=== task: predict easy
title: Three inserts at the front
code:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

head = None
for x in [1, 2, 3]:
    head = Node(x, head)
print(head.val, head.next.val, head.next.next.val)
```
wrong[1 2 3]: Each new node goes in front of the others, so the last one inserted, 3, ends up first.
wrong[3 1 2]: 2 went in front of 1, and then 3 went in front of 2: 3, 2, 1.
hint: After the first insert, the list holds only 1.
hint: Then 2 goes in front of 1, and 3 in front of 2.
right: 3, 2, 1: inserting at the front reverses the order. Each insert is O(1).
wrong: The list goes 1, then 2 → 1, then 3 → 2 → 1. The head is the last node made.

=== task: multi easy
title: O(1) with only a head
prompt: A singly linked list keeps only its `head`. Choose **every** operation that is O(1).
* Insert at the front :: Right. Make a node that links to the head, and make it the head.
* Delete the first node :: Right. `head = head.next`.
* Read the first item :: Right. `head.val`.
* Insert after a node you're holding :: Right. Two links change.
- Read item i :: That means following i links from the head: O(i).
- Add at the end :: Without a `tail`, you walk the whole list to find the last node: O(n).
hint: Does it need to walk along the list?
hint: Four of them don't.
right: Anything at the head, or next to a node you hold, is O(1). Anything that has to find a place first needs a walk: O(n).
wrong: O(1): insert at the front, delete the first node, read the first item, insert after a held node. Reading item i and adding at the end need a walk.

=== task: parsons easy
title: Add up the values
prompt: Arrange the lines so that `total` adds up the values in a linked list, and the program prints:

```output
15
```

Not every line is needed.
lines:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, next=None):
        self.val = val
        self.next = next

def total(head):
    result = 0
    node = head
    while node is not None:
        result += node.val
        node = node.next
    return result

print(total(Node(5, Node(8, Node(2)))))
```
distractors:
```python
    while node.next is not None:
        result = node.val
```
hint: Start at the head, and stop when `node` is None.
hint: Add `node.val`, then move on with `node = node.next`.
right: 5 + 8 + 2 = 15: every node is visited once, O(n). `while node.next is not None` would stop one node early and miss the 2.
wrong: Set `result = 0` and `node = head`; while `node is not None`, add `node.val` and move to `node.next`; then return the result.

=== task: cells easy
title: Follow the addresses
prompt: Each row is a node in memory: its address, its value, and the address of the next node. The head is at address 500. What are the items, in list order?

| Address | Value | Next |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 120 | 6 | None |
| 300 | 1 | 120 |
| 500 | 3 | 840 |
| 840 | 9 | 300 |
labels: item 0 | item 1 | item 2 | item 3
answer: 3 | 9 | 1 | 6
wrong[6 | 1 | 3 | 9]: That's the order of the addresses. A linked list's order comes from its links: start at the head, 500, and follow Next.
wrong[3 | 9 | 6 | 1]: Node 840 links to 300, which holds 1. The node at 120 comes last: its Next is None.
hint: Item 0 is at the head, address 500.
hint: Node 500 links to 840, and node 840 links to 300.
right: 500 → 840 → 300 → 120 gives 3, 9, 1, 6: scattered in memory, in order through the links.
wrong: Start at 500 (3), go to 840 (9), then 300 (1), then 120 (6), whose Next is None.

=== task: mcq easy
title: On repeat
prompt: A music app plays a playlist on repeat: after the last song, the first one plays again. Which kind of linked list fits this best?
* A circular linked list :: Right. The last node links back to the first, so the songs come round again by themselves.
- A singly linked list :: Its last node's `next` is None, so the playlist would stop after the last song.
- A doubly linked list :: It can walk backwards, which helps a "previous song" button, but on its own it still ends after the last song.
- An array sorted by title :: Sorting doesn't make it repeat, and nothing here needs the songs in order of title.
hint: What should come after the last song?
hint: Which kind has no end?
right: In a circular list the last node links to the first, so "next song" always has an answer. A circular doubly linked list would give "previous song" too.
wrong: Repeating means the last song links back to the first: that's a circular linked list.

